we are less than a week away from the  scheduled summit between President Trump  and North Korean Korean leader Kim  jong-un the US and the West want North  Korea to abandon its nuclear weapons  program North Korea might want the u.s.  to pull all or some of its troops out of  South Korea  tonight national security correspondent  Jennifer Griffin looks at that  relationship and what those changes  might mean defense secretary Jim mattis  on route back from a defense conference  in Singapore spent much of his time  reassuring allies that the US would not  be discussing the removal of US military  forces from Korea in exchange for North  Korea shuttering its nuclear program  it's not even a subject  the president tweeted today quote  meeting in Singapore with North Korea  will hopefully be the start of something  big we will soon see some South Koreans  suggest such a grand bargain should be  on the table Michael O'Hanlon of the  Brookings Institution suggests such a  discussion needs to occur but not at  this summit these conversations are  starting partly because President Trump  has said some things that make people  wonder if he might be willing to make  this kind of a grand bargain and we know  that he's not as enamored of some of  America's alliances as his predecessors  the u.s. maintains twenty 4915 active  duty troops in South Korea in the event  of a war the u.s. is committed by treaty  to send up to 690 thousand additional US  forces to reinforce South Korea along  with 160 naval vessels and 2,000  military aircraft South Korea has the  largest standing ready military of any  of America's 60 allies but even that  army is half the size of North Korea's  conventional forces the Heritage  Foundation's James Califano says it is  cheaper to keep US troops in South Korea  than bring them home Japan and South  Korea bear much of the sustainment costs  of facilities in Northeast Asia so  American taxpayer doesn't pay for all  this stuff the White House announced the  location of the summit today the  Cappella Hotel on sentosa island in  Singapore a five-star resort the meeting  will begin at 9:00 a.m.  even that took days to negotiate Brett  Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon  Jennifer thank you
 

